1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,600
Launch day for the Cheese App.

2
00:00:01,920 --> 00:00:03,925
Last week it ran on a laptop.

3
00:00:04,245 --> 00:00:08,064
This morning it runs in the cloud,
inside a private network.

4
00:00:08,384 --> 00:00:10,688
Within an hour, three alarms go off.

5
00:00:10,933 --> 00:00:12,042
First alarm.

6
00:00:12,362 --> 00:00:15,946
Chat answers that call an outside
language model time out.

7
00:00:16,266 --> 00:00:20,299
Yet the saved chat history,
read from the database, loads fine.

8
00:00:21,033 --> 00:00:22,164
Second alarm.

9
00:00:22,484 --> 00:00:26,366
The page loads for every visitor,
but the Predict button fails.

10
00:00:26,686 --> 00:00:29,673
On the developer's laptop,
the same button worked.

11
00:00:30,417 --> 00:00:31,462
Third alarm.

12
00:00:31,782 --> 00:00:36,262
At 9 in the morning traffic doubles,
and the autoscaler adds replicas.

13
00:00:36,582 --> 00:00:39,222
The newest ones fail
with too many connections,

14
00:00:39,222 --> 00:00:41,019
while their processors sit idle.

15
00:00:41,867 --> 00:00:44,555
And one problem nobody has noticed yet.

16
00:00:44,875 --> 00:00:48,331
The production key is sitting
inside the container image.

17
00:00:48,567 --> 00:00:52,599
Each alarm is one mechanism
from Lectures 10 and 16.

18
00:00:52,919 --> 00:00:56,586
We go from addresses,
to routes and NAT, to the browser,

19
00:00:56,586 --> 00:01:00,449
to configuration, to capacity,
and finally to the database.

20
00:01:00,683 --> 00:01:04,758
The gray tag, top right, names
the slides behind each idea.

21
00:01:05,078 --> 00:01:06,763
Nothing here is a live run.

22
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:09,344
Start on the laptop.

23
00:01:09,664 --> 00:01:16,422
In the course app,
uvicorn starts FastAPI inside a container,

24
00:01:16,422 --> 00:01:19,413
on host 0.0.0.0, port 9000.

25
00:01:19,733 --> 00:01:22,357
That host means listen on every interface.

26
00:01:22,677 --> 00:01:25,378
Bound to 127.0.0.1 instead,

27
00:01:25,378 --> 00:01:30,080
it would answer only
from inside the container.

28
00:01:30,817 --> 00:01:33,868
The container is sealed off
until you publish a port.

29
00:01:34,188 --> 00:01:40,310
-p 9000:9000 is the tunnel
from the laptop's door into the container's.

30
00:01:40,950 --> 00:01:45,943
So the README says
the app is served at localhost:3200,

31
00:01:45,943 --> 00:01:49,483
and it calls the API at localhost:9000.

32
00:01:49,803 --> 00:01:53,921
Localhost always means this machine:
the one the code runs on.

33
00:01:54,167 --> 00:01:58,284
An IP address picks the machine,
and a port picks the service on it.

34
00:01:58,604 --> 00:02:10,508
Addresses that start with 10, with 192.168,
or with 172.16 through 172.31 are private.

35
00:02:10,828 --> 00:02:12,705
The internet does not route them.

36
00:02:12,950 --> 00:02:15,126
Move the same container into the cloud.

37
00:02:15,446 --> 00:02:20,630
Inside the private network it gets
the address 10.0.2.5.

38
00:02:20,950 --> 00:02:23,554
A packet from the internet cannot reach it:

39
00:02:23,554 --> 00:02:26,582
nobody out there has a route
to a private address.

40
00:02:26,817 --> 00:02:28,630
Lecture 10 sums it up.

41
00:02:28,950 --> 00:02:31,617
On the laptop,
your router keeps the world out.

42
00:02:31,937 --> 00:02:35,271
In the cloud,
the public finds you by a DNS name,

43
00:02:35,271 --> 00:02:39,489
a firewall starts closed,
and a load balancer lets traffic in.

44
00:02:39,733 --> 00:02:43,362
The browser asks a resolver
for api.example.com,

45
00:02:43,362 --> 00:02:46,385
the resolver asks the authoritative DNS,

46
00:02:46,385 --> 00:02:50,165
and the load
balancer's public address comes back.

47
00:02:50,485 --> 00:02:55,008
Then the browser connects
to the load balancer on port 443.

48
00:02:55,250 --> 00:02:58,712
The load balancer ends the HTTPS connection

49
00:02:58,712 --> 00:03:02,738
and sends its own request
to the task's port 8000.

50
00:03:03,058 --> 00:03:08,221
There, a security group allows only
the load balancer, on port 8000.

51
00:03:08,541 --> 00:03:12,035
A stranger's packet bounces,
and the reply goes back out,

52
00:03:12,035 --> 00:03:14,855
because the firewall
remembers the connection.

53
00:03:15,100 --> 00:03:18,769
SSH reaches the machine,
while clients reach the service.

54
00:03:19,089 --> 00:03:21,073
The lecture draws Amazon names.

55
00:03:21,393 --> 00:03:24,591
On Google Cloud the neighbors
are Compute Engine,

56
00:03:24,591 --> 00:03:26,876
Cloud Load Balancing and Cloud NAT.

57
00:03:27,117 --> 00:03:31,277
If the question says localhost, think:
the machine the code runs on.

58
00:03:31,597 --> 00:03:35,458
A 10.x address:
private, unreachable from the internet.

59
00:03:35,778 --> 00:03:40,686
Public clients:
a DNS name, the load balancer on 443,

60
00:03:40,686 --> 00:03:43,650
then the private target on 8000.

61
00:03:45,783 --> 00:03:47,160
Now the first alarm:

62
00:03:47,160 --> 00:03:50,946
the language model times out,
but the database answers.

63
00:03:51,266 --> 00:03:58,071
A VPC is one block of addresses,
here 10.0.0.0/16.

64
00:03:58,391 --> 00:04:01,122
The slash counts the fixed bits out of 32.

65
00:04:01,442 --> 00:04:08,098
/16 leaves 16 bits to vary:
65,536 addresses.

66
00:04:08,418 --> 00:04:11,724
/24 leaves 8: 256.

67
00:04:11,966 --> 00:04:14,313
The lecture cuts it into three subnets.

68
00:04:14,633 --> 00:04:16,787
A public one for the load balancer.

69
00:04:17,107 --> 00:04:20,201
A private app subnet for the FastAPI tasks.

70
00:04:20,521 --> 00:04:22,974
A private data subnet for the database.

71
00:04:23,216 --> 00:04:25,243
Every subnet has a route table.

72
00:04:25,563 --> 00:04:28,677
Both send the VPC's own block
to the local route.

73
00:04:28,997 --> 00:04:35,013
The only difference is the default row,
0.0.0.0/0.

74
00:04:35,333 --> 00:04:38,285
A public subnet sends it
to the Internet Gateway,

75
00:04:38,285 --> 00:04:40,454
a private subnet to the NAT Gateway.

76
00:04:40,700 --> 00:04:43,708
A route alone is not enough, slide 13 adds:

77
00:04:43,708 --> 00:04:47,974
the machine also needs a public address
and an open firewall.

78
00:04:48,216 --> 00:04:53,842
A packet for the database
at 10.0.3.7 matches both rows,

79
00:04:53,842 --> 00:04:58,563
and the most specific match wins:
/16 beats /0.

80
00:04:58,883 --> 00:05:01,699
The packet stays inside, on the local route.

81
00:05:02,433 --> 00:05:06,987
A packet for the language model
API matches only the default row,

82
00:05:06,987 --> 00:05:09,089
so it goes to the NAT Gateway.

83
00:05:09,409 --> 00:05:12,910
NAT rewrites the private source
to its own public address,

84
00:05:12,910 --> 00:05:16,108
writes the mapping in its notebook,
and sends it out.

85
00:05:17,083 --> 00:05:21,072
The reply comes back, NAT finds the line,
and hands it to the task.

86
00:05:21,392 --> 00:05:25,062
A packet nobody asked for has no line,
so it is dropped.

87
00:05:25,382 --> 00:05:28,240
NAT only carries connections
that start inside.

88
00:05:28,560 --> 00:05:32,699
Nothing uninvited gets in,
which keeps the private subnet private.

89
00:05:32,933 --> 00:05:34,192
Now NAT fails.

90
00:05:34,512 --> 00:05:37,178
Every call to the internet
stalls at the dead hop.

91
00:05:37,498 --> 00:05:40,701
But the database packet
still matches the local route,

92
00:05:40,701 --> 00:05:43,429
and the load balancer
still reaches the tasks.

93
00:05:43,749 --> 00:05:45,285
That is the first alarm.

94
00:05:45,533 --> 00:05:46,685
First question.

95
00:05:47,005 --> 00:05:50,310
A worker in a private subnet
downloads model weights

96
00:05:50,310 --> 00:05:52,431
from a public website at startup,

97
00:05:52,431 --> 00:05:55,965
and nobody on the internet
may open a connection to it.

98
00:05:56,285 --> 00:05:58,141
Where should its default route point?

99
00:05:58,461 --> 00:05:59,826
Pause here if you want.

100
00:06:10,066 --> 00:06:11,066
C.

101
00:06:11,154 --> 00:06:13,634
NAT carries the worker's request out,

102
00:06:13,634 --> 00:06:17,319
maps the reply back,
and refuses uninvited connections.

103
00:06:17,639 --> 00:06:22,815
A, the Internet Gateway, would download too,
but it makes the subnet public,

104
00:06:22,815 --> 00:06:26,152
a direct path in,
guarded only by firewall rules.

105
00:06:26,400 --> 00:06:30,481
B fails: the local route
covers only the VPC's own block,

106
00:06:30,481 --> 00:06:32,629
and the website is outside it.

107
00:06:32,949 --> 00:06:37,045
D fails: a load balancer brings
requests in to its targets.

108
00:06:37,365 --> 00:06:38,816
It is not a way out.

109
00:06:39,650 --> 00:06:44,258
If the question says private subnet, think:
the default route goes to NAT.

110
00:06:44,578 --> 00:06:47,437
NAT only carries connections
that start inside.

111
00:06:47,757 --> 00:06:50,469
And if NAT is down, internet calls fail,

112
00:06:50,469 --> 00:06:54,604
while traffic inside the VPC,
like the database, still works.

113
00:06:56,750 --> 00:06:59,331
Now the second alarm, the failing button.

114
00:06:59,651 --> 00:07:01,486
Loading a page is two trips.

115
00:07:01,806 --> 00:07:07,630
First the browser downloads
HTML, CSS and JavaScript from a static host.

116
00:07:07,950 --> 00:07:11,599
Then that JavaScript runs
on the visitor's own machine,

117
00:07:11,599 --> 00:07:12,793
and calls the API.

118
00:07:13,033 --> 00:07:15,614
So the browser lives outside the VPC.

119
00:07:15,934 --> 00:07:17,810
Static files never enter it,

120
00:07:17,810 --> 00:07:21,697
and every API request must come
in through the front door,

121
00:07:21,697 --> 00:07:22,889
the load balancer.

122
00:07:23,133 --> 00:07:24,306
Now the course app.

123
00:07:24,626 --> 00:07:25,870
In development,

124
00:07:25,870 --> 00:07:31,263
the frontend takes its API address
from NEXT_PUBLIC_BASE_API_URL,

125
00:07:31,263 --> 00:07:34,376
and that variable says localhost:9000.

126
00:07:34,696 --> 00:07:38,856
On the developer's laptop,
that was exactly where the API ran.

127
00:07:39,100 --> 00:07:43,175
Ship that to a visitor,
and localhost means the visitor's laptop.

128
00:07:43,495 --> 00:07:46,161
Nothing listens there, so the request fails.

129
00:07:46,481 --> 00:07:48,103
That is the second alarm.

130
00:07:48,350 --> 00:07:54,510
Swap in the private address, 10.0.2.5,
and the request never reaches the VPC:

131
00:07:54,510 --> 00:07:59,870
that address means something
only inside the visitor's own network.

132
00:08:00,190 --> 00:08:05,609
The public HTTPS name works:
DNS, the load balancer, then a task.

133
00:08:06,600 --> 00:08:09,331
The production file says just /api.

134
00:08:09,651 --> 00:08:13,128
A relative path goes back
to the host that served the page.

135
00:08:13,448 --> 00:08:19,172
That works when one public front
door serves both the page and the API,

136
00:08:19,172 --> 00:08:23,283
the lecture's layout B,
and routes /api to FastAPI.

137
00:08:23,517 --> 00:08:26,061
Slide 24 writes that rule twice,

138
00:08:26,061 --> 00:08:30,194
as an Nginx proxy_pass
or a load balancer path rule.

139
00:08:30,514 --> 00:08:35,570
With a separate static host, the code
must name the public API address instead.

140
00:08:35,817 --> 00:08:41,278
Notice where the private address lives:
in the proxy's config, inside the VPC.

141
00:08:41,598 --> 00:08:43,113
Never in browser code.

142
00:08:43,350 --> 00:08:44,481
Two more facts.

143
00:08:44,801 --> 00:08:49,621
NEXT_PUBLIC_ variables are compiled
into the JavaScript at build time,

144
00:08:49,621 --> 00:08:54,166
so every visitor downloads them:
public by design, never a secret.

145
00:08:54,486 --> 00:08:56,354
And NAT does not help here:

146
00:08:56,354 --> 00:08:59,883
it only carries traffic
that starts inside the VPC.

147
00:09:00,133 --> 00:09:04,062
If the question says code
that runs in the browser, think:

148
00:09:04,062 --> 00:09:05,552
it is outside the VPC.

149
00:09:05,872 --> 00:09:11,226
Call the public HTTPS endpoint,
never localhost or a 10.x address.

150
00:09:11,546 --> 00:09:13,275
NAT does not help inbound.

151
00:09:15,417 --> 00:09:18,425
Now the quiet problem,
the key inside the image.

152
00:09:18,745 --> 00:09:20,644
First, how a release should work.

153
00:09:20,964 --> 00:09:24,110
Build the image once,
push it with a version tag,

154
00:09:24,110 --> 00:09:27,065
and every deployment pulls
that exact version.

155
00:09:27,385 --> 00:09:28,473
No rebuild.

156
00:09:29,384 --> 00:09:31,112
The image is immutable.

157
00:09:31,432 --> 00:09:34,859
It moves from development
to staging to production,

158
00:09:34,859 --> 00:09:36,808
and its digest never changes.

159
00:09:37,128 --> 00:09:42,060
What differs is the runtime values
plugged in when the container starts:

160
00:09:42,060 --> 00:09:45,554
LOG_LEVEL=debug in development,
info in production.

161
00:09:45,800 --> 00:09:47,656
Secrets follow the same rule.

162
00:09:47,976 --> 00:09:50,897
A secret manager delivers the key
at startup,

163
00:09:50,897 --> 00:09:53,949
only to the workload
that is authorized for it.

164
00:09:54,269 --> 00:09:58,878
Never place secrets in source code,
in container images, or in Git.

165
00:09:59,117 --> 00:10:01,613
The course already does the laptop version.

166
00:10:01,933 --> 00:10:05,595
The run script mounts the secrets folder
at /secrets,

167
00:10:05,595 --> 00:10:11,192
and points GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS
at the key's path inside the container.

168
00:10:11,434 --> 00:10:16,362
The project and the ChromaDB host arrive
as environment variables too.

169
00:10:16,682 --> 00:10:20,031
The app reads them with os.environ
when it starts.

170
00:10:20,300 --> 00:10:21,573
The README says why:

171
00:10:21,573 --> 00:10:24,439
we do not want any secure
information in Git.

172
00:10:24,759 --> 00:10:28,258
The key lives next to the repository,
never inside it.

173
00:10:28,500 --> 00:10:29,716
Now the wrong way.

174
00:10:30,036 --> 00:10:34,281
Bake the key into the image, and anyone
who can pull the image can read it.

175
00:10:34,601 --> 00:10:36,485
Rotating it means a rebuild,

176
00:10:36,485 --> 00:10:41,130
and per environment images mean
what you tested is not what you ship.

177
00:10:41,367 --> 00:10:42,604
Second question.

178
00:10:42,924 --> 00:10:45,922
A teammate bakes an API key
into the Dockerfile

179
00:10:45,922 --> 00:10:49,239
and pushes the image
to the team's private registry.

180
00:10:49,559 --> 00:10:51,010
Which statement is true?

181
00:10:51,330 --> 00:10:52,695
Pause here if you want.

182
00:11:02,934 --> 00:11:03,934
D.

183
00:11:04,043 --> 00:11:08,285
The image's settings and layers
are readable by anyone who pulls it,

184
00:11:08,285 --> 00:11:10,593
and changing the key means a rebuild.

185
00:11:10,913 --> 00:11:15,480
A, the private registry,
is still a long list of people and machines,

186
00:11:15,480 --> 00:11:17,995
and the image is copied to every host.

187
00:11:18,234 --> 00:11:21,129
B fails:
the key is written in the Dockerfile,

188
00:11:21,129 --> 00:11:23,269
which is in Git, and in the image.

189
00:11:23,589 --> 00:11:25,957
Ignoring a different file changes nothing.

190
00:11:26,277 --> 00:11:31,332
C fails too: development images leak,
and separate images break build once.

191
00:11:32,167 --> 00:11:36,084
If a question asks how
to move an image to production, think:

192
00:11:36,084 --> 00:11:38,332
the same image, promoted unchanged.

193
00:11:38,652 --> 00:11:39,863
Environment values

194
00:11:39,863 --> 00:11:43,026
and authorized secrets
are injected at startup.

195
00:11:43,346 --> 00:11:46,738
Never rebuild per environment,
and never bake a secret.

196
00:11:48,883 --> 00:11:50,291
Now the third alarm.

197
00:11:50,611 --> 00:11:54,264
An autoscaler measures a signal,
like processor use,

198
00:11:54,264 --> 00:11:56,442
applies a policy with a target,

199
00:11:56,442 --> 00:12:00,446
a minimum and a maximum,
and asks for that many replicas.

200
00:12:00,766 --> 00:12:03,347
But downstream limits
still bound the result.

201
00:12:03,583 --> 00:12:07,572
The database accepts a fixed number
of connections, here 100.

202
00:12:07,892 --> 00:12:12,223
Other clients already hold some:
an admin console, a batch job.

203
00:12:12,466 --> 00:12:14,962
Each replica opens its own connections.

204
00:12:15,282 --> 00:12:21,021
In the course app, every API process
creates its own database client at import.

205
00:12:21,341 --> 00:12:25,501
That client is ChromaDB,
standing in here for a connection pool.

206
00:12:25,750 --> 00:12:27,733
The autoscaler adds replicas,

207
00:12:27,733 --> 00:12:32,107
each plugging its pool into free slots,
until the slots run out.

208
00:12:32,427 --> 00:12:36,054
The next replica cannot connect,
though its processor is idle.

209
00:12:36,300 --> 00:12:38,156
So read the rule off the picture.

210
00:12:38,476 --> 00:12:42,487
100 slots,
minus 10 for the other clients, leaves 90.

211
00:12:42,807 --> 00:12:45,985
Divide by 20 per replica: 4.5.

212
00:12:46,305 --> 00:12:50,237
A replica cannot open half a pool,
so round down to 4,

213
00:12:50,237 --> 00:12:53,004
and set 4 as the autoscaler's maximum.

214
00:12:53,250 --> 00:12:56,976
To go higher,
add a connection pooler or shrink each pool,

215
00:12:56,976 --> 00:12:58,903
or buy a bigger database tier.

216
00:12:59,223 --> 00:13:01,655
Raising the maximum alone does not help.

217
00:13:01,900 --> 00:13:03,052
Third question.

218
00:13:03,372 --> 00:13:05,895
Each replica opens 15 connections,

219
00:13:05,895 --> 00:13:10,049
other clients hold 30,
and at peak you need 10 replicas.

220
00:13:10,369 --> 00:13:12,972
Which is the smallest database tier
that works?

221
00:13:13,292 --> 00:13:14,657
Pause here if you want.

222
00:13:24,900 --> 00:13:26,244
B, 200.

223
00:13:26,564 --> 00:13:31,748
Ten pools of 15 make 150,
plus 30 others: 180.

224
00:13:32,068 --> 00:13:33,753
That fits under 200.

225
00:13:34,000 --> 00:13:38,203
A, 150, holds the pools
but forgets the other clients.

226
00:13:38,523 --> 00:13:42,405
C, 250, works,
but costs more than it needs to.

227
00:13:42,725 --> 00:13:47,674
D: a higher maximum adds no connections,
so the extra replicas fail.

228
00:13:48,516 --> 00:13:52,289
If the question gives a database
connection limit, think:

229
00:13:52,289 --> 00:13:54,299
the limit minus other clients,

230
00:13:54,299 --> 00:13:57,583
divided by connections per replica,
rounded down.

231
00:13:57,903 --> 00:13:59,506
And set it as the maximum,

232
00:13:59,506 --> 00:14:02,959
because the autoscaler
does not know the database limit.

233
00:14:05,100 --> 00:14:07,852
The last mechanism is where the data lives.

234
00:14:08,172 --> 00:14:09,623
Classify it first.

235
00:14:09,943 --> 00:14:12,804
Users and orders, linked by foreign keys,

236
00:14:12,804 --> 00:14:17,409
saved in transactions that are all
or nothing: that is relational.

237
00:14:17,729 --> 00:14:21,420
Find the five most similar cheeses:
that is vector search.

238
00:14:21,667 --> 00:14:25,230
Today the course runs ChromaDB
as its own container.

239
00:14:25,550 --> 00:14:29,634
It is a vector store with metadata,
and the team operates it:

240
00:14:29,634 --> 00:14:32,312
patches, backups and recovery are yours.

241
00:14:32,550 --> 00:14:37,222
The lecture compares three homes:
ChromaDB on a virtual machine,

242
00:14:37,222 --> 00:14:41,894
a PostgreSQL service with pgvector,
and a vector search service.

243
00:14:42,214 --> 00:14:44,006
Vector chips land everywhere.

244
00:14:44,326 --> 00:14:48,763
Foreign keys and transactions
bounce off the two vector-only homes.

245
00:14:49,000 --> 00:14:50,941
You could run two databases.

246
00:14:51,261 --> 00:14:53,122
But then they must stay in sync,

247
00:14:53,122 --> 00:14:56,301
and one day an order exists in one
while its embedding

248
00:14:56,301 --> 00:14:57,832
is missing from the other.

249
00:14:58,067 --> 00:15:02,035
Only PostgreSQL
with pgvector takes every chip.

250
00:15:02,355 --> 00:15:07,293
The vector column sits beside
the relational columns in the same table,

251
00:15:07,293 --> 00:15:11,187
and one query filters with SQL
and orders by similarity.

252
00:15:11,433 --> 00:15:14,804
Managed means the provider runs the hosts
and the patching.

253
00:15:15,124 --> 00:15:19,369
Your team still owns the schema,
queries, pooling and restore tests.

254
00:15:19,689 --> 00:15:24,809
On Google Cloud:
Cloud SQL for PostgreSQL, or AlloyDB.

255
00:15:25,050 --> 00:15:31,112
If the question says relational and vector
needs in one managed database, think:

256
00:15:31,112 --> 00:15:33,007
PostgreSQL plus pgvector.

257
00:15:33,327 --> 00:15:37,381
Foreign keys, transactions
and similarity search, in one place.

258
00:15:39,517 --> 00:15:41,117
Seven rules for the quiz.

259
00:15:41,437 --> 00:15:44,830
One:
localhost is the machine the code runs on,

260
00:15:44,830 --> 00:15:47,069
and 10.x addresses are private.

261
00:15:47,389 --> 00:15:50,866
The public reaches a DNS name
and a load balancer.

262
00:15:51,100 --> 00:15:54,399
Two:
a VPC is an address block cut into subnets,

263
00:15:54,399 --> 00:15:56,668
and the most specific route wins.

264
00:15:56,988 --> 00:16:00,231
Three: NAT only carries connections
that start inside.

265
00:16:00,551 --> 00:16:04,647
If it fails, internet calls fail,
and the database still answers.

266
00:16:04,884 --> 00:16:10,879
Four: the browser is outside the VPC,
so it calls the public HTTPS endpoint.

267
00:16:11,199 --> 00:16:15,572
Five: build one image, and inject settings
and secrets when it starts.

268
00:16:15,817 --> 00:16:20,529
Six: maximum replicas
is the connection limit minus other clients,

269
00:16:20,529 --> 00:16:23,028
divided by each pool, rounded down.

270
00:16:23,348 --> 00:16:27,590
Seven: relational plus vectors
in one managed database

271
00:16:27,590 --> 00:16:30,025
means PostgreSQL with pgvector.

272
00:16:30,267 --> 00:16:33,083
Here is the whole picture,
every alarm fixed.

273
00:16:33,403 --> 00:16:37,696
Static files from the edge,
requests through the load balancer,

274
00:16:37,696 --> 00:16:39,903
tasks in the private app subnet,

275
00:16:39,903 --> 00:16:43,558
PostgreSQL in the data subnet,
and NAT for calls out.

276
00:16:43,800 --> 00:16:47,021
When a question looks hard,
do what the lecture does last.

277
00:16:47,341 --> 00:16:48,941
Trace the request first.

278
00:16:49,261 --> 00:16:50,606
Good luck on the quiz.

